  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.38.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.38.0 closes the last two engine debts that had a provisional way out but no definitive one, and both reach a distribution directly because both are about a distribution's own installed state. First: a single invalid entry hand-written into the journal's granted_directories used to make the whole file unreadable, which blocked the nine commands that read it -- including the directory revoke you would use to remove the entry. Those entries are now quarantined: kept exactly as read, written back unchanged on save, and granted to nothing. The fail-closed refusal that stops a privilege escalation from being replayed did not move; what moved is where it lands, from 'this file cannot be read' to 'this entry grants nothing'. Tolerating the field as empty stays rejected, because an empty field is omitted at serialization and would be silently deleted on the next save. A granted_directories that is not a list at all is still fatal. Second: a new command -- repair under whatever program name the distribution renders -- removes those quarantined entries on an explicit request, in one write, and removes the empty directories that doctor names and that pruning never reached on installations older than 5.28.0. It carries --dry-run and takes a snapshot before writing, and it proves a directory is empty at the moment of removal rather than trusting the earlier scan. Alongside them, doctor stopped reporting a silent all-clear over a configuration directory it could not walk because it is a symlink: it now names what it could not reach, and repair does not claim success over ground it never walked. An adversarial review ran the scenarios instead of reading the diff and found two real defects -- a containment filter that a '..' segment walked straight through, and a loop clause no test could distinguish from the structural fact that already guaranteed it -- both fixed before this release. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
}
